#{[17592186567077 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."] [17592186567082 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,3]\\times [-3,-1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-y+5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point $[1,\\,-2]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration.\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$4$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$2$\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n$-4\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$2.25$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-4}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186567082 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-y+5) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-y+5)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2xy & {x}^{2}-1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2xy & {x}^{2}-1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-y+5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2xy & {x}^{2}-1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-y+5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4(1)^{3} & -2(-2) \\\\ 2(1)(-2) & {1}^{2}-1\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{4}-(-2)^{2}+1 \\\\ (1)^{2}(-2)-(-2)+5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=\\begin{bmatrix}1.25 \\\\ -0.75\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+\\begin{bmatrix}1.25 \\\\ -0.75\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1.25 \\\\ -0.75\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2.25 \\\\ -2.75\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186567092 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-3,0]\\times [-3,0]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+3 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-2x+5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[-2,\\,-2]^{T}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n            {{field-1}}\n        </td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n            {{field-2}}\n        </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186567092 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-2x+5) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-2x+5)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2xy-2 & {x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2xy-2 & {x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+3 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-2x+5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2xy-2 & {x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+3 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-2x+5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4(-2)^{3} & -2(-2) \\\\ 2(-2)(-2)-2 & (-2)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}(-2)^{4}-(-2)^{2}+3 \\\\ (-2)^{2}(-2)-2(-2)+5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}-32 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{(-32)\\cdot 4-4\\cdot 6}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -4 \\\\ -6 & -32\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{152}\\begin{bmatrix}56 \\\\ -122\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}-1.632 \\\\ -2.803\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [17592186567100 :question/problem "Let \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $det(A)=0$\n\nII. The system  $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution\n\nIII. $A$ is invertible"] [17592186567100 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-(-3)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is false, all the other parts of the theorem are false too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore only statements I is true."] [17592186567104 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,0]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3x{y}^{2}-2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,1]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[-1.5,\\,1]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{2}≈[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186567104 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-3x{y}^{2}-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-3x{y}^{2}-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x & -2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2} & -6xy\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2x & -2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2} & -6xy\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3x{y}^{2}-2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2x & -2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2} & -6xy\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3x{y}^{2}-2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1.5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2(-1.5) & -2(1) \\\\ 3(-1.5)^{2}-3(1)^{2} & -6(-1.5)(1)\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}(-1.5)^{2}-(1)^{2}-1 \\\\ (-1.5)^{3}-3(-1.5)(1)^{2}-2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1.5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 3.75 & 9\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0.25 \\\\ -0.875\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1.5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{(-3)\\cdot 9-(-2)\\cdot 3.75}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 2 \\\\ -3.75 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.25 \\\\ -0.875\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1.5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{19.5}\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 \\\\ 1.6875\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}-1.474 \\\\ 1.087\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [17592186567109 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{1}=[$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">$]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186567109 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3}-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-2x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-2x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x & -3{y}^{2} \\\\ -2 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2x & -3{y}^{2} \\\\ -2 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2x & -3{y}^{2} \\\\ -2 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2(-1) & -3(1)^{2} \\\\ -2 & 2(1)\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}(-1)^{2}-(1)^{3}-1 \\\\ (1)^{2}-2(-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot (-2)}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.3 \\\\ 0.2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186674190 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 6 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 6 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 6 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=16.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v=u^{2} \\\\ v=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 4,16).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-4\\le u\\le 4,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 16\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/38/387e22e062c4ff7f24d26561fe369041f11f59a2b0305f8dc7b3ce49d411031a.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-6\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{16}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[v]_{u^{2}}^{16}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 16 u]_{-4}^{4} \\\\ &=1(((64)+(- \\frac{64}{3}))-((-64)+(\\frac{64}{3}))) \\\\ &=1(\\frac{256}{3}) \\\\ &=\\frac{256}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186674193 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=4.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v=u^{2} \\\\ v=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2,4).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/b4/b43c84eb76241233dc88e2d6a94bc4e5e24d558f8c9f46a826d0d928059aefc2.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-1\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{4}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}[v]_{u^{2}}^{4}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}(4)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}(4 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 4 u]_{-2}^{2} \\\\ &=1(((8)+(- \\frac{8}{3}))-((-8)+(\\frac{8}{3}))) \\\\ &=1(\\frac{32}{3}) \\\\ &=\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186674204 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the 